<title>BELGIUM: EU ministers expected to note flower threat.</title>
<headline>EU ministers expected to note flower threat.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-03-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Farm ministers are next week expected to accept a report on the impact third country trade agreements can have on the European Union's flower and plant trade, an EU member state official said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The report was accepted by the special committee on agriculture on Tuesday.</p>
<p>It reflects the concern among EU flower and plant growers about the effect preferential trade agreements have on their livelihoods, the official explained.</p>
<p>&quot;The report was accepted by the committee and ministers are expected to ask for it to be drawn to the attention of officials negotiating third country preferential trade agreements. There is no proposal for action to adopt,&quot; the official said.</p>
</text>
